atchRatio":0.48200000000000004},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.48},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.47450000000000003},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.4693},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.4661},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4646},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.4585},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4531},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.449},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4459},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.44049999999999995},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.4325},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.4233},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.41100000000000003},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.3969},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3908}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":165,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"The teachers in 1800 Bengal were mostly Dalits.  This isn't a modern claim.  It's this own testimony of a British missionary, William Ward.  A man who arrived in India to convert Hindus but was stabbed by the system he came to dismantle.  Ward conducted a massive survey of Bengal's indigenous schools long before any English influence.  He said that these teachers were generally respectable Shudras.  Teaching was a noble profession, funded by the community and respected by everyone from the Brahmin to the Dalit.  It was a thriving organic system.  But then the British took over.  They Did n't Reform It.  [MUSIC] They executed it.  They cut every single funding lifeline from temples through free land grants and replace the local vernacular schools with mostly missionary- run English schools.  Why the Truth Is Brutal.  This education system which once empowered every class was disenfranchised by design to create obedient subjects, not educated citizens.  A Legacy Destroyed Not by Need But by a Chilling Imperial Calculation."}}